{"name":"Fragrant Jasmine","hex":"#fbf6e7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fragrant","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Sweet-smelling; having a pleasant (usually strong) scent or fragrance.","Attractive."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fragrant"},{"term":"jasmine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several plants, of the genus Jasminum, mostly native to Asia, having fragrant white or yellow flowers.","The perfume obtained from these plants."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jasmine"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chrysojasminum odoratissimum","blocks":[{"text":"Chrysojasminum odoratissimum, the fragrant jasmine, is a species of flowering plant in the family Oleaceae. It is native to Madeira and the Canary Islands. A scrambling evergreen climber reaching 2.5 m (8 ft), under its synonym Jasminum odoratissimum it has gained the Royal Horticultural Society's Award of Garden Merit as a wall shrub."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Jasminum_odoratissimum_2c.JPG/330px-Jasminum_odoratissimum_2c.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chrysojasminum_odoratissimum"},{"title":"Fragrance compound","blocks":[{"text":"A fragrance compound is a chemical compound with a pleasant odor. Fragrances affect only the sense of smell, whereas flavors can affect both the sense of taste and smell. Fragrances are often mixtures of individual fragrance compounds. Although many fragrances are derived from natural sources, many are synthetic. Fragrances are widely used in cosmetics and are the basis for a large industry."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fragrance_compound"},{"title":"Jasmine","blocks":[{"text":"Jasmine is a genus of shrubs and vines in the olive family of Oleaceae. It contains around 200 species native to tropical and warm temperate regions of Asia, Africa, and Oceania. Jasmines are widely cultivated for the characteristic fragrance of their flowers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Common_Jasmine.jpg/330px-Common_Jasmine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jasmine"},{"title":"Jasmine (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The color jasmine is a pale tint of yellow, displayed at right. It is a representation of the average color of the more yellowish lower part of the pale yellowish white colored jasmine flower. The first recorded use of jasmine as a color name in English was in 1925."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Jsambac.jpg/330px-Jsambac.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jasmine_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Jasmine_%28Jasminum_odoratissimum%29_%283504773944%29.jpg/500px-Jasmine_%28Jasminum_odoratissimum%29_%283504773944%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Jasmine_%28Jasminum_odoratissimum%29_%283504773944%29.jpg/500px-Jasmine_%28Jasminum_odoratissimum%29_%283504773944%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJasmine_(Jasminum_odoratissimum)_(3504773944).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Perfume_shelf_536pix.jpg/500px-Perfume_shelf_536pix.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Perfume_shelf_536pix.jpg/500px-Perfume_shelf_536pix.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fragrance bottles","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APerfume_shelf_536pix.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/